1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,855
A kitchen scale weighs sugar in grams.

2
00:00:02,855 --> 00:00:05,157
A jeweller weighs gold in milligrams.

3
00:00:05,157 --> 00:00:07,188
But how do you weigh one atom?

4
00:00:07,588 --> 00:00:12,483
One hydrogen atom has a mass of about
one point six seven times ten to

5
00:00:12,483 --> 00:00:15,024
the power minus twenty four grams.

6
00:00:15,474 --> 00:00:17,386
Diya looks at her kitchen scale.

7
00:00:17,386 --> 00:00:19,748
It could never show a number that small.

8
00:00:20,098 --> 00:00:23,050
So chemists built a new
scale, just for atoms.

9
00:00:23,550 --> 00:00:26,587
By the end, you will weigh
any molecule in seconds.

10
00:00:27,237 --> 00:00:28,963
Why do we need a new unit?

11
00:00:29,313 --> 00:00:31,963
Write the hydrogen atom's mass in grams.

12
00:00:31,963 --> 00:00:36,169
A decimal point, then twenty
three zeros, then one six seven.

13
00:00:36,519 --> 00:00:38,846
Numbers like this are hard to use.

14
00:00:38,846 --> 00:00:41,979
So we compare every atom
with one standard atom.

15
00:00:42,579 --> 00:00:45,395
Let's build that scale
on our molecule bench.

16
00:00:45,395 --> 00:00:48,951
The standard is one kind of
carbon atom, carbon twelve.

17
00:00:49,301 --> 00:00:51,921
Split its mass into twelve equal parts.

18
00:00:51,921 --> 00:00:55,012
One part is the atomic
mass unit, written u.

19
00:00:55,412 --> 00:00:59,658
So one u is one twelfth of the
mass of one carbon twelve atom.

20
00:01:00,058 --> 00:01:02,747
Now weigh hydrogen against these parts.

21
00:01:02,747 --> 00:01:07,402
It balances just over one part,
about one point zero zero eight u.

22
00:01:07,802 --> 00:01:11,809
An oxygen sixteen atom
balances almost sixteen parts.

23
00:01:11,809 --> 00:01:14,370
Fifteen point nine nine five u.

24
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:17,626
Here is the math behind it, step by step.

25
00:01:17,976 --> 00:01:18,931
Step one.

26
00:01:18,931 --> 00:01:24,414
One u equals one point six six zero
five six times ten to the minus twenty

27
00:01:24,414 --> 00:01:25,486
four grams.

28
00:01:26,236 --> 00:01:27,158
Step two.

29
00:01:27,158 --> 00:01:32,221
One hydrogen atom has a mass of one
point six seven three six times ten

30
00:01:32,221 --> 00:01:34,651
to the minus twenty four grams.

31
00:01:35,401 --> 00:01:36,386
Step three.

32
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:38,764
Divide the atom's mass by one u.

33
00:01:38,764 --> 00:01:40,691
The powers of ten cancel.

34
00:01:41,441 --> 00:01:42,345
Step four.

35
00:01:42,345 --> 00:01:45,554
That leaves one point
zero zero seven eight u.

36
00:01:46,304 --> 00:01:49,344
But atoms of one element
can differ in mass.

37
00:01:49,344 --> 00:01:52,100
Chlorine has two kinds, called isotopes.

38
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:56,835
Chlorine thirty five has a mass
of thirty four point nine seven u.

39
00:01:56,835 --> 00:02:00,379
Chlorine thirty seven, thirty
six point nine seven u.

40
00:02:00,779 --> 00:02:05,416
In nature, seventy five point five
percent are the lighter kind, and twenty

41
00:02:05,416 --> 00:02:07,753
four point five percent the heavier.

42
00:02:08,153 --> 00:02:09,368
Pause and think.

43
00:02:09,368 --> 00:02:14,187
Is chlorine's average atomic mass closer
to thirty five, or to thirty seven?

44
00:02:17,387 --> 00:02:18,898
Closer to thirty five!

45
00:02:18,898 --> 00:02:23,298
Most atoms are the lighter kind, so
they pull the average toward them.

46
00:02:23,948 --> 00:02:27,155
Multiply each mass by its
share, and add the two.

47
00:02:27,905 --> 00:02:32,630
That gives thirty five point four
six, about thirty five point five u.

48
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:34,886
This is the average atomic mass.

49
00:02:35,536 --> 00:02:36,532
Remember this.

50
00:02:36,532 --> 00:02:38,545
Atoms are not weighed in grams.

51
00:02:38,545 --> 00:02:40,836
They are compared with carbon twelve.

52
00:02:41,536 --> 00:02:43,849
Now join atoms into molecules.

53
00:02:43,849 --> 00:02:49,320
Molecular mass is the sum of the atomic
masses of all atoms in a molecule.

54
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:52,777
Methane is one carbon and four hydrogens.

55
00:02:52,777 --> 00:02:58,047
Twelve point zero one one, plus four
times one point zero zero eight, is

56
00:02:58,047 --> 00:03:00,531
sixteen point zero four three u.

57
00:03:01,131 --> 00:03:02,866
Now, one worked example.

58
00:03:03,166 --> 00:03:07,653
Find the molecular mass of
glucose, C six H twelve O six.

59
00:03:07,653 --> 00:03:13,136
Carbon is twelve point zero one one
u, hydrogen one point zero zero eight,

60
00:03:13,136 --> 00:03:14,418
oxygen sixteen.

61
00:03:14,918 --> 00:03:16,045
Six carbons.

62
00:03:16,045 --> 00:03:21,564
Six times twelve point zero one one
is seventy two point zero six six u.

63
00:03:22,314 --> 00:03:23,670
Twelve hydrogens.

64
00:03:23,670 --> 00:03:28,825
Twelve times one point zero zero
eight is twelve point zero nine six u.

65
00:03:29,575 --> 00:03:30,814
Six oxygens.

66
00:03:30,814 --> 00:03:33,842
Six times sixteen is ninety six u.

67
00:03:34,592 --> 00:03:35,645
Add them up.

68
00:03:35,645 --> 00:03:38,650
One hundred and eighty
point one six two u.

69
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:42,355
But some compounds have
no molecules at all.

70
00:03:42,355 --> 00:03:44,273
Watch sodium meet chlorine.

71
00:03:44,673 --> 00:03:48,539
Each sodium atom gives one
electron to a chlorine atom.

72
00:03:48,539 --> 00:03:51,450
They become charged particles called ions.

73
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:57,351
The ions pack together, each sodium
ion surrounded by six chloride ions.

74
00:03:57,351 --> 00:03:59,460
No single molecule exists.

75
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:02,693
So we use the formula unit, N a C l.

76
00:04:02,693 --> 00:04:07,411
Its formula mass is twenty three plus
thirty five point five, fifty eight

77
00:04:07,411 --> 00:04:08,524
point five u.

78
00:04:09,174 --> 00:04:10,639
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:04:10,989 --> 00:04:15,815
Students count only one hydrogen in
water, and get seventeen point zero

80
00:04:15,815 --> 00:04:16,995
zero eight u.

81
00:04:17,395 --> 00:04:20,372
But the small two means
two hydrogen atoms.

82
00:04:20,372 --> 00:04:25,209
Two times one point zero zero eight,
plus sixteen, is eighteen point zero

83
00:04:25,209 --> 00:04:26,109
two u.

84
00:04:26,552 --> 00:04:28,086
Where is this asked?

85
00:04:28,086 --> 00:04:32,698
As the first step of almost every
mole numerical, and as average atomic

86
00:04:32,698 --> 00:04:33,851
mass questions.

87
00:04:34,451 --> 00:04:35,722
Exam level now.

88
00:04:35,722 --> 00:04:40,731
An element has isotopes of mass ten
u and eleven u, at twenty and eighty

89
00:04:40,731 --> 00:04:41,631
percent.

90
00:04:41,496 --> 00:04:43,651
Find its average atomic mass.

91
00:04:43,651 --> 00:04:44,976
Pause and solve.

92
00:04:50,376 --> 00:04:51,682
The answer is C.

93
00:04:51,682 --> 00:04:53,090
Ten point eight u.

94
00:04:53,590 --> 00:04:55,874
Multiply each mass by its share.

95
00:04:55,874 --> 00:04:58,221
Ten times zero point two is two.

96
00:04:58,721 --> 00:05:01,822
Eleven times zero point
eight is eight point eight.

97
00:05:02,322 --> 00:05:05,242
Two plus eight point
eight is ten point eight.

98
00:05:05,242 --> 00:05:09,231
B is the plain average, but the
heavier isotope is more common.

99
00:05:09,831 --> 00:05:11,292
Here is your recap.

100
00:05:11,292 --> 00:05:12,793
Screenshot this card.

101
00:05:15,993 --> 00:05:18,991
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

102
00:05:19,391 --> 00:05:20,291
One.

103
00:05:19,865 --> 00:05:22,165
What is one u, in your own words?

104
00:05:22,965 --> 00:05:23,865
Two.

105
00:05:23,473 --> 00:05:27,070
Why is the atomic mass of
chlorine not a whole number?

106
00:05:27,870 --> 00:05:28,770
Three.

107
00:05:28,583 --> 00:05:31,819
Find the molecular mass of carbon dioxide.

108
00:05:32,719 --> 00:05:36,326
Next time: how many molecules
are in one sip of water?

109
00:05:36,326 --> 00:05:39,184
We find out in mole
concept and molar mass.
